---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
---
<BaseLayout current="NASA Research" title="NASA Research" description="A curated gateway to NASA's public research, engineering methods, software, datasets and robotics resources.">
  <div class="eyebrow">RESEARCH FIELD GUIDE / 01</div>
  <h1>NASA Research</h1>
  <p class="lede">A practical gateway to the knowledge, methods, software and technical work NASA makes available to the public.</p>
  <div class="prose intro">
    <p>This page is a working map for learning how complex engineering systems are researched, designed, tested and improved. It brings together technical publications, systems engineering methods, open-source projects, software and scientific data.</p>
    <p>The aim is not simply to read about space technology. It is to study the engineering process behind it, understand the architecture, inspect the tools, reproduce useful methods and adapt what is transferable to robotics, advanced manufacturing and industrial systems.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="callout"><span class="eyebrow">A RESEARCH WORKFLOW</span><p><strong>Find evidence → define the problem → understand requirements → study the architecture → model and simulate → prototype and test → document what you learn.</strong></p><p>Use this sequence as a guide, not as a rigid checklist. Real engineering work is iterative.</p></div>

  <section class="resource-section">
    <div class="section-index">01 / TECHNICAL LITERATURE</div><div class="section-content">
      <h2>Read the research</h2>
      <p>Start with technical reports, conference papers, journal articles and documented experiments. Search by a specific engineering question rather than browsing without direction.</p>
      <div class="resource"><a href="https://ntrs.nasa.gov/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">NASA Technical Reports Server (NTRS) ↗</a><p>NASA's technical publication repository. Search reports and papers on robotics, autonomous systems, materials, structures, manufacturing and testing.</p></div>
      <div class="resource"><a href="https://ntrs.nasa.gov/search?q=robotic%20assembly" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">NTRS — Robotic assembly search ↗</a><p>A direct starting search for robotic assembly literature. Refine the query as your research question becomes more precise.</p></div>
      <div class="resource"><a href="https://ntrs.nasa.gov/search?q=additive%20manufacturing%20materials" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">NTRS — Additive manufacturing and materials ↗</a><p>Explore material-process relationships, manufacturing methods and technical evaluation.</p></div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="resource-section">
    <div class="section-index">02 / ENGINEERING METHODS</div><div class="section-content">
      <h2>Learn how NASA engineers work</h2>
      <p>These references help turn an interesting idea into a system with requirements, interfaces, risks and verifiable performance.</p>
      <div class="resource"><a href="https://www.nasa.gov/reference/systems-engineering-handbook/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">NASA Systems Engineering Handbook ↗</a><p>A foundational guide to lifecycle thinking: system design, requirements, architecture, technical management, risk, integration, verification and validation.</p></div>
      <div class="resource"><a href="https://www.nasa.gov/reference/4-0-system-design-processes/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">System Design Processes ↗</a><p>A focused entry into the design side of systems engineering, from needs and requirements toward a system solution.</p></div>
      <div class="resource"><a href="https://swehb.nasa.gov/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">NASA Software Engineering Handbook ↗</a><p>Practices and guidance for developing and assuring software used in complex systems.</p></div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="resource-section">
    <div class="section-index">03 / ROBOTICS & AUTONOMY</div><div class="section-content">
      <h2>Study real robotic systems</h2>
      <p>Use documented missions and research platforms to understand sensing, localization, mobility, manipulation, planning, control and simulation.</p>
      <div class="resource"><a href="https://github.com/nasa/astrobee" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">NASA Astrobee — Open-source robot software ↗</a><p>Software for free-flying robots aboard the International Space Station. A useful codebase to explore autonomy, navigation, localization, simulation and robot-system integration.</p></div>
      <div class="resource"><a href="https://www.nasa.gov/intelligent-systems-division/autonomous-systems-and-robotics/intelligent-robotics-group/software-and-datasets/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Intelligent Robotics Group — Software and datasets ↗</a><p>NASA robotics software and datasets that can support study and experimentation.</p></div>
      <div class="resource"><a href="https://www.nasa.gov/reference/jsc-robotics/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Johnson Space Center Robotics ↗</a><p>An overview of robotics work at JSC, including technologies and applications for challenging environments.</p></div>
      <div class="resource"><a href="https://github.com/NASA-JSC-Robotics" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">NASA JSC Robotics on GitHub ↗</a><p>Explore public code repositories associated with NASA Johnson Space Center robotics.</p></div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="resource-section">
    <div class="section-index">04 / SOFTWARE & OPEN SOURCE</div><div class="section-content">
      <h2>Inspect tools and code</h2>
      <p>Reading code and software documentation is a way to understand how engineering ideas become working tools.</p>
      <div class="resource"><a href="https://software.nasa.gov/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">NASA Software Catalog ↗</a><p>A catalogue of NASA software. Check each item's release category, license, access conditions and permitted uses before downloading or reusing it; availability is not the same as unrestricted permission.</p></div>
      <div class="resource"><a href="https://github.com/nasa" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">NASA on GitHub ↗</a><p>Public repositories spanning science, engineering, visualization, data and software.</p></div>
      <div class="resource"><a href="https://code.nasa.gov/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">NASA Open Source Projects ↗</a><p>Information about NASA's open-source development activities and projects.</p></div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="resource-section">
    <div class="section-index">05 / DATA & SCIENCE</div><div class="section-content">
      <h2>Work with real data</h2>
      <p>Scientific datasets can become the basis for analysis, visualization, models and independent research projects.</p>
      <div class="resource"><a href="https://data.nasa.gov/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">NASA Open Data ↗</a><p>Discover public datasets and data resources published by NASA.</p></div>
      <div class="resource"><a href="https://science.data.nasa.gov/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">NASA Science Data ↗</a><p>Access science-focused data resources and services.</p></div>
      <div class="resource"><a href="https://earthdata.nasa.gov/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">NASA Earthdata ↗</a><p>Earth science data and tools, potentially useful for environmental, climate and geospatial investigations.</p></div>
      <div class="resource"><a href="https://api.nasa.gov/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">NASA APIs ↗</a><p>APIs that make selected NASA data and services accessible to software projects.</p></div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="resource-section">
    <div class="section-index">06 / LEARNING</div><div class="section-content">
      <h2>Learn and practice</h2>
      <p>Use educational resources to build foundations, then connect them to technical papers and practical work.</p>
      <div class="resource"><a href="https://www.nasa.gov/learning-resources/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">NASA Learning Resources ↗</a><p>Educational materials and learning activities across NASA topics.</p></div>
      <div class="resource"><a href="https://www.nasa.gov/learning-resources/nasa-stem-opportunities-activities/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">NASA STEM Opportunities and Activities ↗</a><p>STEM activities, challenges and opportunities. Check eligibility and participation rules because some programmes are restricted by country or institution.</p></div>
      <div class="resource"><a href="https://science.nasa.gov/learn/professional-learning/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">NASA Professional Learning ↗</a><p>Professional learning resources and educational material for deeper study.</p></div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="resource-section">
    <div class="section-index">07 / RESEARCH PRACTICE</div><div class="section-content">
      <h2>Questions to investigate</h2>
      <p>These are starting queries for a personal research programme. Narrow each one into a question that can be answered with evidence, a model or an experiment.</p>
      <ul>
        <li>How can modular robots assemble structures from repeatable building blocks?</li>
        <li>How can robots coordinate manipulation and construction in constrained environments?</li>
        <li>How can low-cost sensors improve material characterization during manufacturing?</li>
        <li>How can open-source hardware and software make experimental engineering more accessible?</li>
        <li>Which NASA methods can transfer to industrial inspection, maintenance and hazardous environments?</li>
      </ul>
      <div class="topic-list"><span>Modular robotics</span><span>Autonomous systems</span><span>Robotic manipulation</span><span>Materials science</span><span>Additive manufacturing</span><span>Systems engineering</span><span>Simulation & control</span><span>Testing & validation</span></div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <div class="callout final-note"><span class="eyebrow">HOW TO USE THIS LIBRARY</span><p>For every resource, record the question you are investigating, the source, the method, the evidence, the limitations and the next experiment or action. A useful research note should make it possible to return to an idea and continue working.</p><p><a class="text-link" href="/knowledge/">Return to the Knowledge Library →</a></p></div>
</BaseLayout>
<style>
